    <string name="help_title">Help</string>
    <string name="help_open">How it works</string>
    <string name="help_s1_t">What this is</string>
    <string name="help_s1_b">EasyLink is the companion app for EasyBridge devices. The phone connects to an EasyBridge box over Bluetooth (BLE); boxes talk to each other over LoRa radio. Works in forests and mountains — no cellular, no internet, no SIM.\n\nChain: your phone → BLE → your EasyBridge → LoRa air → peer\'s EasyBridge → BLE → their phone.</string>
    <string name="help_s2_t">What LoRa is</string>
    <string name="help_s2_b">LoRa is 433/868 MHz radio: very long range (kilometers to tens of km line-of-sight) but very slow — hundreds of bytes per second. Messages are short and not instant.\n\nTwo speed presets:\n• LONG RANGE (SLOW) — max distance, a text takes seconds;\n• FAST — shorter range, quicker (needed for voice notes).\n\nSpeed switches for the WHOLE network at once: the device broadcasts an announce and everyone flips together after ~6 s. Nodes on different presets cannot hear each other.\n\nThe network is a mesh: every device relays others\' packets (up to 8 hops).</string>
    <string name="help_s3_t">Messages, DMs and groups</string>
    <string name="help_s3_b">Everything on LoRa air is broadcast. The general channel is heard by everyone.\n\nDMs and groups are labels on top of the shared air: other apps don\'t display such messages, but technically the radio signal reaches every device with the same firmware. Real privacy comes from air encryption (PSK): set the same passphrase on all YOUR devices in Settings → Security.\n\nTicks: one — sent to air, two blue — the peer acknowledged (ACK).\nLimits: text up to ~1200 bytes, voice notes up to 8 s.</string>
    <string name="help_s4_t">Calls (radio)</string>
    <string name="help_s4_b">A call is walkie-talkie mode: half-duplex, one talks, others listen. Hold TALK while speaking.\n\nModes: LONG RANGE (muffled, max distance), STANDARD, HD (best audio, least range).\n\nCalls only work on SX127x boards (e.g. LilyGO T3). During a call the device leaves LoRa for FSK — messages don\'t flow meanwhile.\n\nTargeted calls (one person instead of everyone) need firmware V2.9.1+ on both devices; with older firmware everyone hears the call.</string>
    <string name="help_s5_t">Device differences</string>
    <string name="help_s5_b">• LilyGO T3 (SX1276) — full package: radio calls, display, 433 MHz.\n• ESP32-S3 + E220 — UART module: messages only, no calls.\n• ESP32-C6 + E220 — same, smaller.\n• ESP32-S3 + E22 (SX1268) — strong RF path, calls not field-tested yet.\n\nTX power is adjustable: 21–30 dBm. Antennas: stock whip — all directions; Yagi — far in one direction (use Aim in the node card).</string>
    <string name="help_s6_t">SOS</string>
    <string name="help_s6_b">The SOS button sends an alarm 3 times at the longest-range speed. SOS bypasses blocklists and rate limits; every phone in the network sounds a siren and shows your name. Use responsibly.\n\nIf location broadcasting is on, friends will see you on the map.</string>
    <string name="help_s7_t">Range tips</string>
    <string name="help_s7_b">1. Height wins: raise the device, climb a hill.\n2. Line of sight beats watts: forest and buildings eat the signal.\n3. LONG RANGE preset + 30 dBm.\n4. Yagi antenna + the Aim screen: follow the arrow, watch the signal trace.\n5. Drop a relay node on high ground — the mesh will route through it.</string>

